// Formative practice quiz for merge-vs-rebase. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
// Objective o1 also names fast-forward merges; the topic page does not teach them, so no item tests them.
$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/git-foundations/merge-vs-rebase

::merge-vs-rebase o1 - shape of a merge::feature has your commit C3; since you branched at C2, main gained C4. On feature you run git merge main. What does feature's history look like now?{
=C3 is kept as it was, and a new merge commit with two parents, C3 and C4, is on top#Correct. A merge keeps every existing commit and joins the two lines with a merge commit.
~C3 is replaced by a new copy that sits on top of C4#Not quite. That is what git rebase main does. A merge never replaces commits.
~C4 is copied onto feature as a new commit, and no merge commit is made#Not quite. A merge of diverged histories adds a merge commit with two parents; it does not copy commits.
~main moves forward to the new merge commit too#Not quite. A merge moves only the branch you are on, here feature. main stays at C4.
}

::merge-vs-rebase o2 - commit IDs after a rebase::feature has commits C5 and C6. You run git rebase main on feature. A teammate compares commit IDs before and after. What do they find?{
=New commits C5' and C6' with the same changes and new IDs sit on main's tip; the old ones are abandoned#Correct. Rebase replays each commit on the new base, which creates new commits with new IDs.
~C5 and C6 have the same IDs; they were only moved#Not quite. A commit's ID depends on its parent, so replaying it on a new base makes a new commit.
~C5 and C6 were squashed into one commit#Not quite. A plain rebase replays each commit separately; it does not combine them.
~A merge commit with two parents was added#Not quite. That is what git merge main does. Rebase makes a straight line with no merge commit.
}

::merge-vs-rebase o3 - when rebasing is fine::Your feature commits exist only on your laptop; you have never pushed them. main has moved on, and you want a straight-line history. Is git rebase main acceptable?{
=Yes. Nobody else has these commits, so replacing them affects no one#Correct. The rule is never to rebase commits other people already have. Commits only you have are safe to rebase.
~No. Rebase is never allowed; always merge#Not quite. Rebase is a normal tool. It is risky only for commits others already have.
~No. Rebase loses your changes#Not quite. Rebase replays your changes as new commits; the changes themselves are kept.
~Yes, but push the commits first so they are backed up#Not quite. Pushing first is what makes them shared. Rebase while they exist only on your laptop.
}

::merge-vs-rebase o3 - a teammate rebased shared work::A teammate rebased the shared branch dev after you had pulled it and added your own commits on top. What problem do you now have?{
=Your commits sit on the old commits that the rebase replaced, so your history no longer matches theirs#Correct. Rebase made new commits with new IDs. Your work still builds on the abandoned ones, which is why commits others have must not be rebased.
~None. The rebase only changed their laptop, so nothing differs#Not quite. Once they share the rebased branch, its commits differ from the ones your work is built on.
~Your own commits were deleted from your laptop#Not quite. Their rebase cannot delete anything on your computer; the trouble is that the histories no longer match.
~Git updates your commits to the new base automatically on the next pull#Not quite. Git does not fix this for you; someone has to sort out the mismatched history by hand.
}
